WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Good morning Hank; it's Monday.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.560
Nerdfighters often ask us if we're religious,
a topic we've been reluctant to discuss over

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.210
the years mostly because the quality of discourse
about religion on the internet is...atrocious.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.370
That said, many nerdfighters know that a decade
ago I worked as a Chaplin at a children's

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:17.330
hospital. And I was also briefly enrolled
in, although I actually never attended, the

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.250
University of Chicago's divinity school. So
today I'm going to share everything I have

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.030
to say about religion. Hopefully that will
lead to a productive and thoughtful conversation,

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:28.820
although given the history of the internet
and conversations about religion...probably

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.820
not.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.630
Okay Hank, so I would submit that almost all
human lives are usually lived in a state of,

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:37.000
like, functional nihilism. Very few of us
believe that human life is devoid of all meaning

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.720
and that all we should do is answer to our
base urges and fulfill our basic desires and

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:47.140
try to distract ourselves from pain or fear
or unpleasantness, but almost all of us act

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.550
as if we believe that. Like I have this massive
parallel processing unit that sits on top

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:55.860
of my neck that I could use to, like, contribute
to cancer research or write a novel or puzzle

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.780
through quantum mechanics. And I have this
amazing body that I could use to plant trees

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.430
or fight wild fires or harvest food for the
poor.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.539
Hank, remember that time I said I have an
amazing body? I don't mean that I have an

00:01:06.539 --> 00:01:10.170
amazing body. I mean everybody has... it's
amazing what our bodies can do.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.029
But I don't usually do any of that stuff.
Hank, most of my free time is spent, like,

00:01:13.029 --> 00:01:16.590
watching television, playing video games,
or searching for lols.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:20.700
Christians, Muslims, atheists, whatever, all
of us share this worldview; almost none of

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.880
us would abandon our comforts to help others;
almost all of us would acknowledge that much

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.209
of our time each day is wasted.

00:01:28.209 --> 00:01:32.169
In short, Hank, if we just judge humans by
their actions, we look like a bunch of nihilists.

00:01:32.169 --> 00:01:37.270
But we aren't nihilists because we all feel
called to make the world better. To understand

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:42.609
and observe the universe. To make art, to
bring beautiful things into life. To contribute

00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:44.439
in some way to the human experiment.

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:48.640
Everybody wants their lives to matter, to
have meant something. That call to meaning

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.090
is the foundation of religious worldviews,
but it's also the foundation of successful

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.569
secular worldviews which is why I don't really
care to debate the existence of God with people.

00:01:56.569 --> 00:01:58.969
Like, Hank, imagine you wake up in the middle
of the night and your house is covered in

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:03.159
smoke and you hear an invisible voice shout
"Your house is on fire!” Hank, in that situation

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:06.479
I would find it, like, totally uninteresting
to have a discussion about where that voice

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:10.929
was coming from and how it got in the house
and was it God or a fireman. I would just,

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:12.019
you know, leave.

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:15.519
In short, Hank, for me at least, debating
the mere existence of God is a way of avoiding

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:20.439
the deeper and more devastating question of
how we are going to bring meaning to human

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:21.439
life.

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:24.570
I think that's, broadly speaking, the point
of religion. Religion is essentially a response

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:28.650
to revelation. And that revelation almost
always calls us to embrace meaning, or, if

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.709
it doesn't innately exist, then to create
meaning. But that's also true for atheistic

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:37.659
humanism. We are all facing the same question:
what are we going to do with our consciousness?

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:42.200
Will we seek to fight poverty or climate change
or disease or war or spend all our resources

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.390
ensuring that we have a beautifully manicured
lawn with absolutely no weeds?

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:49.849
Sorry, I'm on a bit of a lawn kick at the
moment because my next door neighbor is not

00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:53.780
terribly happy with me for leaving the country
for two months during the, uh, lawn growing

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.150
season. It's just grass, dude! Actually...
it's not just grass. That's his problem. It's

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.379
also a lot of dandelions.

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:01.510
So what are we going to do about consciousness
and how are we going to balance our urge to

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:06.360
be more than nihilists with our need to blunt
the sharp edges of consciousness with funny

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.109
stuff like Paula Deen riding Paula Deen riding
me or Humpy Hank?

00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:13.719
Those are interesting questions, Hank, and
Nerdfighteria has been talking about those

00:03:13.719 --> 00:03:17.609
questions for years now. Some of us through
a religious lens, some of us through a secular

00:03:17.609 --> 00:03:20.909
lens, but the lens doesn't interest me as
much as the questions.

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:24.480
Sorry this video didn't contain many jokes.
To make up for it, here is a baby rhinoceros.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.599
Also, as penance, I will eat 36 disgusting
sandwiches on Friday.

00:03:28.599 --> 00:03:31.769
Hank, don't forget to be awesome. I will see
you on Wednesday.

